-- Digital Foundations: from zero to hero.
-- A plain-English course on how the internet, browsers, APIs, apps, design,
-- data and security fit together, written for people working on a digital
-- transformation of council repairs who are not from a technical background.
--
-- Safe to run more than once: if a course with this title already exists the
-- whole script does nothing (and glossary terms use INSERT IGNORE so existing
-- definitions are never overwritten). Requires sql/schema.sql to have been run.
-- Diagrams referenced as local:*.svg live in assets/img/learning/.

SET @exists := (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM pm_learning_courses WHERE title = 'Digital Foundations: From Zero to Hero');
INSERT INTO pm_learning_courses (title, description, sort_order)
SELECT 'Digital Foundations: From Zero to Hero', 'A plain-English tour of how the digital world actually works: the internet, browsers, APIs and their methods (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE), apps, databases, design, data and security. Written for people working on council repairs transformation who are experts in their own field but not in technology. Every lesson has something to try for yourself in your own browser, plus short checks and a scenario at the end of each module. No prior knowledge assumed.', (SELECT COALESCE(MAX(c.sort_order), 0) + 1 FROM pm_learning_courses c)
FROM DUAL WHERE @exists = 0;
SET @course := IF(@exists = 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);

-- ===== Module 1: Start Here: What "Digital" Really Means =====
INSERT INTO pm_learning_modules (course_id, title, description, sort_order)
SELECT @course, 'Start Here: What "Digital" Really Means', 'Before any jargon: what digital actually is, how it differs from "doing things on a computer", and a map of the whole territory so everything later has somewhere to land.', 1 FROM DUAL WHERE @course > 0;
SET @mod := IF(@course > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);

INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Digital in plain English', 'What "digital" means, and the difference between digitising, going digital and transforming.', '## You do not need to become technical\n\nLet us start with reassurance. This course is not trying to turn you into a developer. It is trying to give you the **mental map** that technical people carry around in their heads, so that when a supplier, a developer or a consultant says "we\'ll expose that through an API and cache it at the edge", you can follow the conversation, ask a good question, and spot when something does not add up.\n\nMost people working on a repairs transformation already have the hard part: they understand the work. Some know repairs inside out, others know how the council runs, pays and governs itself. Technology is the missing third leg, and it is much less mysterious than it sounds.\n\n## What does "digital" actually mean?\n\nAt its simplest, **digital** means information stored as numbers (strictly, as ones and zeros) that a computer can store, copy, search, send and process almost instantly and almost for free. A paper repair form is one physical thing in one place. A digital repair record can be seen by customer support, planners and contractors at the same moment, copied a million times at no cost, searched in a blink and analysed alongside every other repair.\n\nThree words get mixed up constantly, and the difference matters for the Repairs project:\n\n- **Digitisation** is turning something paper into something electronic. Scanning a repair form into a PDF is digitisation. Nothing about the process changes.\n- **Digitalisation** is using digital tools to do an existing process better. Tenants reporting repairs through a web form instead of by phone is digitalisation.\n- **Digital transformation** is rethinking how the service works, using what digital makes possible. A repair that is diagnosed from a photo, booked into the contractor\'s diary automatically, tracked live by the tenant and paid on completion with no invoice is a different service, not a faster version of the old one.\n\nThe Repairs project is a transformation project. That is why it is a change management challenge first and a technology challenge second.\n\n::: repairs\nThink of one repair from report to payment. Which steps involve someone re-typing information that already exists somewhere else? Every one of those is a spot where digital could help, and also where integration between systems will matter.\n:::\n\n## How this course works\n\nEach lesson is short and plain. Almost every one has a **Try it yourself** box: a small thing to do in your own browser to see the idea with your own eyes. Please do these. People remember what they did far better than what they read.\n\nAt the end of each lesson there are a couple of quick checks, and at the end of each module there is a scenario to think through using real repairs knowledge. There are no marks and no one is watching: they exist to help ideas stick.\n\n::: try\nOpen a new browser tab and go to `https://www.gov.scot`. Without clicking anything, write down (in your head is fine) three things you can see on the page that you reckon are being **stored somewhere else and pulled in**: for example, the latest news list. By the end of this course you will be able to explain exactly how that happens.\n:::\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Scottish Government: Digital Strategy](https://www.gov.scot/publications/a-changing-nation-how-scotland-will-thrive-in-a-digital-world/)', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
