INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'The big picture: the layers of a digital service', 'A single map of how a person, a screen, an API, a back end and a database fit together.', '## One diagram to carry through the whole course\n\nAlmost every digital service you will ever meet, from a banking app to the council\'s repair-reporting page, is built from the same handful of layers. If you remember only one picture from this course, make it this one.\n\n![Layers of a digital service: person, front end, API, back end, database](local:stack-layers.svg)\n\nRead it left to right as a story, using a tenant reporting a leaking tap:\n\n1. **The person** opens the council website or app on their phone.\n2. **The front end** is what they see and tap: the buttons, the form, the photo upload. It runs on their own device.\n3. **The API** is the messenger. The front end does not reach into the council\'s systems directly. It sends a tidy, standardised message ("create a repair with these details") to the API.\n4. **The back end** receives it and applies the rules: is this an emergency? Which priority? Which contractor patch is it in? Is the address valid?\n5. **The database** is where the finished repair record is saved, so everyone can see it later.\n\nThe answer travels back the same way: database to back end to API to front end to the person\'s screen ("Your repair reference is R-4821").\n\n## Why this map is so useful\n\nWhen something goes wrong or something needs to change, the first question a technical person asks is **"which layer?"**\n\n- "The button is the wrong colour" is a front-end issue.\n- "Emergency repairs are being given routine priority" is a back-end rules issue.\n- "The contractor\'s app cannot see new jobs" is probably an API or integration issue.\n- "Last year\'s repairs are missing" is a database or data issue.\n\nLearning to ask "which layer?" will save this project hours of confusion and miscommunication.\n\n## Underneath it all: the internet and servers\n\nAll these layers talk to each other over a network, and the back end and database run on **servers**: computers that sit in a data centre (or in "the cloud", which is just someone else\'s data centre) and answer requests around the clock. Later modules open up each box: how the internet carries messages, what a browser really does, what an API is in detail, how databases work, and how all of it is designed, secured and bought.\n\n::: try\nOpen any website you use daily (your online bank, a supermarket, a news site) and try to point to the five layers. What is the front end? What do you think is stored in the database behind it? You cannot see the back end, which is rather the point, but you can guess what rules it applies.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nFront end = what you see. Back end = what you do not. API = the messenger between them. Database = the memory. Servers and the internet = the plumbing underneath.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
