  UNION ALL SELECT 'They stayed the same as in paper days' AS t, 0 AS c, 'The opposite happened: expectations rose.' AS e, 1 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'They became limited to email contact' AS t, 0 AS c, 'The opposite happened: expectations rose.' AS e, 2 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'They were shaped by the best online experiences they had anywhere, not only by other councils' AS t, 1 AS c, 'People compare a council service with their bank or favourite shop, not just with other councils.' AS e, 3 AS s) o WHERE @q > 0;

INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (module_id, kind, format, prompt, model_answer, sort_order)
SELECT @mod, 'scenario', 'reflection', 'A senior colleague says: "Why can\'t our repairs system just talk to the contractor\'s system? They are all on the internet." Drawing on this module (packets, shared protocols, URLs, HTTP), explain in a few sentences why being "on the internet" is necessary but not sufficient for two systems to work together.', 'Being on the internet means the two systems can exchange packets, which is the postal service. It does not mean they understand each other\'s messages. For systems to work together they also need an agreed language and rules on top: the web showed this with URLs (agreed addresses), HTTP (agreed way to ask and answer) and HTML (agreed format for pages). Systems exchanging data need the same sort of thing: an agreed address for each thing, an agreed way to ask, and an agreed data format. That agreement is what an API provides, and where a supplier has not built one (or built one in a different style), someone has to build a translator, which is where cost and delay arise. So "on the internet" gets the packets there; an API and shared standards are what make the conversation meaningful.', 0 FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;

-- ===== Module 3: How the Internet Works =====
INSERT INTO pm_learning_modules (course_id, title, description, sort_order)
SELECT @course, 'How the Internet Works', 'Addresses, names, packets, servers, the cloud and the padlock: what actually happens between your finger and the other side of the world.', 3 FROM DUAL WHERE @course > 0;
SET @mod := IF(@course > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);

INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'The internet versus the web', 'Why these are not the same thing, and what else runs on the internet.', '## The roads and the shops\n\nPeople say "the internet" and "the web" as if they are one thing. They are not, and the difference helps you think clearly about systems.\n\n- **The internet** is the global network of connected computers and cables: the roads.\n- **The web** is one service that travels along those roads: web pages, fetched using HTTP. It is the shops, libraries and offices on the roads.\n\nOther services use the same roads without being "the web": **email** (carried by protocols like SMTP), **video calls** such as Teams, **online gaming**, **streaming**, and most importantly for the Repairs project, **APIs**: software talking directly to software, with no web page involved at all.\n\n## Who owns the internet?\n\nNo one. It is a network of networks, joined by agreement. Your connection comes from an **internet service provider (ISP)** (BT, Virgin, Vodafone and so on), or for a council, often through a public sector network. That ISP connects to others, large "backbone" operators connect those, and undersea cables carry traffic between continents. A handful of organisations set the common standards (for example the IETF for technical standards, and ICANN for coordinating names and numbers), but nobody runs the whole thing.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nWhen someone says "the system is down", it is worth asking: is it the **internet** (a connection problem), the **web server** (the website itself), or the **application** (the software behind it)? Each has a different fix and a different owner. Many arguments between a council and a supplier about whose fault an outage was come down to exactly this distinction.\n\n::: repairs\nContractors working in cellars, stairwells and rural areas often have no signal at all. A mobile repairs app that needs a constant internet connection will fail in the places repairs actually happen. "Works offline and syncs later" is a requirement worth asking suppliers about early.\n:::\n\n::: try\nCheck what kind of connection you are on right now. On your phone, turn Wi-Fi off and on and notice what stops working. Then think: which of your daily apps work with no internet at all, and which need it every second?\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
SELECT @lesson, 'quick_check', 'multiple_choice', 'Which of these uses the internet but is NOT part of the web?', 0 FROM DUAL WHERE @lesson > 0;
SET @q := IF(@lesson > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct, explanation, sort_order)
SELECT @q, o.t, o.c, o.e, o.s FROM (SELECT 'Software calling an API directly, or email' AS t, 1 AS c, 'The web is one service; email and API calls use the same network but not web pages.' AS e, 0 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'Reading a news website' AS t, 0 AS c, 'Those are all web pages, which are the web.' AS e, 1 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'Opening a council web page' AS t, 0 AS c, 'Those are all web pages, which are the web.' AS e, 2 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'Filling in an online form in a browser' AS t, 0 AS c, 'Those are all web pages, which are the web.' AS e, 3 AS s) o WHERE @q > 0;
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
SELECT @lesson, 'quick_check', 'multiple_choice', 'Who owns and runs the internet?', 1 FROM DUAL WHERE @lesson > 0;
SET @q := IF(@lesson > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct, explanation, sort_order)
SELECT @q, o.t, o.c, o.e, o.s FROM (SELECT 'The US government' AS t, 0 AS c, 'No single government or company controls the whole thing.' AS e, 0 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'No single organisation; it is a network of networks joined by shared standards' AS t, 1 AS c, 'Many organisations run parts of it, joined by agreed standards.' AS e, 1 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'Google' AS t, 0 AS c, 'No single government or company controls the whole thing.' AS e, 2 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'The United Nations' AS t, 0 AS c, 'No single government or company controls the whole thing.' AS e, 3 AS s) o WHERE @q > 0;

INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Addresses and names: IP and DNS', 'How a name like gov.scot turns into the numeric address a computer needs.', '## Computers like numbers; people like names\n\nEvery device on the internet has an **IP address**: a number that identifies it, such as `203.0.113.7`. That is the "street address" packets are delivered to. But nobody wants to type numbers. So we use **domain names** like `gov.scot` instead, and a system translates between the two.\n\nThat system is the **DNS** (Domain Name System), often called the phone book of the internet. When you type a web address, your device quietly asks a DNS server "what is the number for gov.scot?", gets an answer, and only then contacts the real server.\n\n![DNS translates a website name into an IP address](local:dns-lookup.svg)\n\n## Reading a web address\n\nA web address (URL) has parts, each with a job. Take `https://www.gov.scot/news/latest`:\n\n- `https` is the **protocol**: how to talk (secure web).\n- `www.gov.scot` is the **domain name**: which server.\n- `.scot` is the **top-level domain**. Others you know: `.uk`, `.com`, `.gov.uk`. The `.scot` one is for Scottish people, organisations and institutions.\n- `/news/latest` is the **path**: which page or resource on that server.\n\nYou will see this structure again in the API module, where the path is how you say *which repair* you want.\n\n## Two flavours of IP address\n\nThe old style, **IPv4**, looks like `203.0.113.7` and there are only about 4.3 billion of them, which ran short years ago. The newer **IPv6** looks like `2001:db8::1` and has an effectively unlimited supply. You do not need to remember the details, only that both are in use.\n\n::: try\n**Look up a real domain.** Go to `https://lookup.icann.org` and enter `gov.scot` (or a council\'s domain). You will see who registered it and which servers answer for it.\n\n**See DNS in action** (if your PC lets you open a command prompt or Terminal): type `nslookup gov.scot` and press Enter. The number you get back is the IP address for that name. If the IT setup blocks this, a website such as `https://dnschecker.org` does the same job.\n:::\n\n::: tip\nWhy does this matter to the Repairs project? Because moving a service to a new supplier often means changing what a domain name points to. It is a small change technically, but the planning around **when** and **who is responsible** is where projects trip up.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
SELECT @lesson, 'quick_check', 'multiple_choice', 'What does DNS do?', 0 FROM DUAL WHERE @lesson > 0;
SET @q := IF(@lesson > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct, explanation, sort_order)
SELECT @q, o.t, o.c, o.e, o.s FROM (SELECT 'Encrypts web traffic' AS t, 0 AS c, 'That is a different job. DNS only translates names into numbers.' AS e, 0 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'Stores web pages for faster loading' AS t, 0 AS c, 'That is a different job. DNS only translates names into numbers.' AS e, 1 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'Translates human-friendly names like gov.scot into numeric IP addresses' AS t, 1 AS c, 'DNS is the internet\'s phone book.' AS e, 2 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'Blocks harmful websites' AS t, 0 AS c, 'That is a different job. DNS only translates names into numbers.' AS e, 3 AS s) o WHERE @q > 0;
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
SELECT @lesson, 'quick_check', 'multiple_choice', 'In the URL https://www.gov.scot/news, which part is the path?', 1 FROM DUAL WHERE @lesson > 0;
SET @q := IF(@lesson > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct, explanation, sort_order)
SELECT @q, o.t, o.c, o.e, o.s FROM (SELECT 'https' AS t, 0 AS c, 'That part identifies something else (protocol, server or top-level domain).' AS e, 0 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT 'www.gov.scot' AS t, 0 AS c, 'That part identifies something else (protocol, server or top-level domain).' AS e, 1 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT '.scot' AS t, 0 AS c, 'That part identifies something else (protocol, server or top-level domain).' AS e, 2 AS s
  UNION ALL SELECT '/news' AS t, 1 AS c, 'The path says which page or resource on that server you want.' AS e, 3 AS s) o WHERE @q > 0;

INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Packets, routers and the journey of a message', 'How data is chopped up, sent by different routes and put back together, and what "latency" and "bandwidth" mean.', '![A fibre-optic cable: each hair-thin glass strand carries data as pulses of light. Photo: ASD, CC BY-SA 4.0](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Optical_fiber_cable-01ASD.jpg/330px-Optical_fiber_cable-01ASD.jpg)\n\n## Everything is cut into packets\n\nWhen you load a web page or send a photo, the data is not sent as one lump. It is cut into thousands of small **packets**, each labelled with where it came from, where it is going and its number in the sequence. They are launched into the network and handed from **router** to router, each one making a quick decision: "which of my neighbours is closest to the destination?"\n\n![A message split into packets travelling via routers](local:packets.svg)\n\nDifferent packets from the same photo may take different routes. At the other end, the receiving computer reassembles them in order, and asks for any that went missing to be re-sent. This is TCP and IP, from the history module, in action.\n\n## Bandwidth and latency: two different things\n\nThese are often confused, and suppliers sometimes use the confusion.\n\n- **Bandwidth** is how much data can flow per second: the width of the pipe. It matters for big downloads and video.\n- **Latency** is how long a single message takes to make the trip: the length of the pipe. It matters for anything interactive, like a video call or a form that must respond immediately.\n\nA satellite connection can have high bandwidth but high latency: great for streaming a film, frustrating for a call. A tenant in a rural Highland area might have a slow connection overall. Services built for a fast office network can feel broken at the other end of a poor mobile signal.\n\n## Why not just one route?\n\nBecause of the resilience lesson from the history module. A message that can take many routes survives broken cables and busy links. It is also why the network is cheap to expand: you add more routers and links and the system adapts.\n\n::: try\n**Trace a real journey.** On a Mac, open Terminal and type `traceroute gov.scot`. On Windows, open Command Prompt and type `tracert gov.scot`. Each line is one router your request passed through, with the time in milliseconds. Count the hops and spot where the time jumps. That jump is often where your traffic crosses the sea or leaves your provider\'s network.\n\nIf you cannot open a command prompt on a council PC, try the website `https://traceroute-online.com` instead.\n\nNext, run `ping gov.scot` (press Ctrl+C to stop on a Mac). The time shown is the **latency** between you and that server.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nTest the repairs app or portal on a poor connection, not only in the office. Ask a colleague to try it on a mobile phone indoors, or in a basement. That is the real experience of many tenants and contractors.\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
INSERT INTO pm_learning_quiz_questions (lesson_id, kind, format, prompt, sort_order)
SELECT @lesson, 'quick_check', 'multiple_choice', 'A video call is jerky even though the connection can download large files quickly. Which is the most likely culprit?', 0 FROM DUAL WHERE @lesson > 0;
