INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Servers, data centres and "the cloud"', 'What a server is, what "cloud" actually means, and who looks after what in IaaS, PaaS and SaaS.', '![A data centre aisle at CERN: rows of servers behind cooled doors. Photo: CERN, CC BY-SA 4.0](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/CERN_Computer_Center_07.jpg/330px-CERN_Computer_Center_07.jpg)\n\n## A server is just a computer with a job\n\nA **server** is a computer whose job is to answer requests from other computers. It does not have to look special: many are modest machines stacked in racks. They are built for reliability, run all day every day, and live in a **data centre**: a secure building with backup power, cooling and many network connections.\n\nEvery website, app and API you use has a server (usually many) behind it somewhere.\n\n## What "the cloud" really means\n\n"The cloud" is a friendly name for **using computers that someone else owns and runs, over the internet**. Instead of buying servers, installing them in the organisation\'s own building and looking after them, you rent capacity from a provider like Microsoft (Azure), Amazon (AWS) or Google, and pay for what you use. Amazon launched its first big cloud services in 2006, and the idea has since reshaped IT.\n\nWhy organisations move to the cloud: they can scale up or down as demand changes, avoid big upfront hardware costs, get stronger resilience and security than most can afford alone, and free staff from maintaining equipment. Why it is not magic: you still pay, you still need good design and governance, and the council\'s data now lives under someone else\'s roof, which raises legal and security questions.\n\n## Who looks after what?\n\nThis is the most useful picture in this lesson. The more you rent as a ready-made service, the less you manage, and the less you control.\n\n![Responsibility split for on-premises, IaaS, PaaS and SaaS](local:cloud-models.svg)\n\n- **On-premises**: the council owns and runs everything, in its own building. Maximum control, maximum burden.\n- **IaaS** (Infrastructure as a Service): you rent raw servers and manage everything above them.\n- **PaaS** (Platform as a Service): the supplier also manages the platform; you only bring your application and data.\n- **SaaS** (Software as a Service): you simply use a finished product through a browser. The supplier runs everything. Microsoft 365 is SaaS. Most modern housing and repairs systems are too.\n\n## Where do the servers live? It matters\n\nPublic sector data has rules about location and security. Councils are expected to consider where data is stored, who can access it, and what happens if a supplier fails or is taken over. Government guidance (the UK\'s "Cloud First" approach and the National Cyber Security Centre\'s cloud security principles) provides a framework for asking the right questions.\n\n::: try\nVisit `https://www.microsoft.com/en-gb/trust-center` or search "Azure UK data centre regions". Notice that cloud providers publish exactly where their data centres are. Ask yourself: if our supplier\'s system is SaaS, do we know which country our tenants\' data sits in?\n:::\n\n::: repairs\n"Is the repairs system SaaS, and if so, where is the data held and who can access it?" is a good question for any supplier. It leads naturally to questions on backups, support hours and what happens to the council\'s data if it ever leaves.\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
