INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Staying safe: HTTPS, encryption and certificates', 'What the padlock really means, and what it does not.', '## The problem with sending things in the open\n\nYour packets pass through many routers owned by many organisations. If they travelled as plain text, anyone along the route could read them or change them: your password, a tenant\'s name and address, a payment. That is why almost all web traffic is now protected.\n\n## HTTPS and the padlock\n\nPlain **HTTP** sends data in the open. **HTTPS** is HTTP wrapped in **encryption**: your browser and the server agree a secret key and scramble everything they send, so that anyone listening in sees only gibberish. The modern protocol that does this is called TLS.\n\nHTTPS also provides proof of identity. When you connect to a site, it presents a **certificate**: a digital document, vouched for by a trusted organisation (a "certificate authority"), saying "this server really is gov.scot". Your browser checks it. If something is wrong, it warns you before you proceed.\n\n## What the padlock does and does not mean\n\nThe padlock means: *the connection to this site is private, and the site is who it says it is.* It does **not** mean the site itself is trustworthy. A scam website can have a perfectly valid padlock for its own scammy address. This is a very common misunderstanding and a favourite of fraudsters, so teach it to colleagues and tenants.\n\n## Three things worth remembering\n\n- **Never enter personal data on a site without HTTPS.** Modern browsers warn you.\n- **Certificates expire** (typically annually or sooner). An expired certificate makes a site show a scary warning and can break integrations. It is a classic cause of "everything stopped working this morning".\n- **Encryption protects data in transit** (while moving). Protecting it **at rest** (while stored) is a separate matter that the supplier must also do.\n\n::: try\n**Inspect a certificate.** Open `https://www.gov.scot`. Click the padlock or the "site information" icon in the address bar, then choose something like "Connection is secure" and then "Certificate is valid". You can see who issued it, who it was issued to and when it expires. Then try `http://neverssl.com`, a site that deliberately uses no encryption, and see how your browser labels it.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nContractors will use the system on personal phones and public Wi-Fi in cafes, vans and tenants\' homes. HTTPS keeps that traffic private, but only if the system enforces it everywhere. When reviewing a supplier, ask: is all traffic encrypted, in transit and at rest?\n:::', 5, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
