INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'HTML, CSS and JavaScript: the three languages of the web', 'Structure, style and behaviour, explained with a house analogy, and how to peek at them in any page.', '## Three languages, three jobs\n\nEvery web page is built from three kinds of code. A house analogy makes them easy to hold in your head:\n\n- **HTML** is the **structure**: the walls, doors and rooms. It says "this is a heading, this is a paragraph, this is a form with a text box".\n- **CSS** is the **style**: the paint, furniture and layout. It says "headings are green, forms are 600 pixels wide, buttons have rounded corners".\n- **JavaScript** is the **behaviour**: the electrics and plumbing. It makes things happen: showing a message when you tick a box, checking your postcode as you type, loading new content without reloading the page.\n\nStrip away the CSS and a page still works but looks like a plain document. Strip away JavaScript and many modern pages stop working properly. Strip away the HTML and there is nothing.\n\n## What HTML looks like\n\nHTML marks up content with **tags** in angle brackets. A tiny repair form:\n\n```\n<h1>Report a repair</h1>\n<form>\n  <label>What is wrong?</label>\n  <input type="text" name="problem">\n  <button>Send</button>\n</form>\n```\n\nYou do not need to write HTML. You do need to be able to see it, because it is the quickest way to understand what a page is made of, and to check what a supplier has really built.\n\n## Why this matters to non-developers\n\n- **Accessibility**: screen readers rely on well-formed HTML. A page that looks fine but is built badly can be unusable for a blind tenant.\n- **Cost of change**: changing a colour is a CSS task (cheap). Changing a rule about what happens when you click "Send" is JavaScript or back-end work (more expensive). Knowing which is which helps you judge quotes.\n- **JavaScript is not Java.** They are unrelated languages that happen to share part of a name for marketing reasons in 1995.\n\n::: try\n**See the code of any page.**\n1. Open `https://www.gov.scot` (or a council\'s website).\n2. Right-click on a blank part of the page and choose **View page source** (or press `Ctrl+U` on Windows, `Option+Cmd+U` on Mac).\n3. Scroll through: that tangle of angle brackets is the HTML. Look for `<h1>`, `<a href=...>` and `<img src=...>`.\n4. Use `Ctrl+F` to search for the word "contact" and find where it appears.\n\n**Now change a page (it is safe).** Right-click on a heading, choose **Inspect**, double-click the heading text in the panel that opens, type your own words and press Enter. The page changes on your screen only. Refresh and it all goes back. You have just edited a website without any special access, and you have learned why "I can see it on my screen" is not the same as "it is published".\n:::\n\n::: tip\nSome council computers have developer tools restricted by policy. If so, try this on a personal device.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
