INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Cookies, sessions and staying logged in', 'How a website remembers you, why logging in is harder than it looks, and what that means for privacy.', '## The web has no memory\n\nHere is a surprising fact: HTTP, the web\'s protocol, is **stateless**. Every request is treated as if it were the first from a stranger. The server has no built-in way to know that the request you just made came from the same person as the one a second ago.\n\nBut you log in once and then browse a whole system. How?\n\n## Cookies and sessions\n\nWhen you log in, the server checks your username and password, creates a **session** (a record that "this person is logged in"), and sends back a small piece of text called a **cookie** with a unique code in it. Your browser stores that cookie and automatically attaches it to every later request to that site. The server reads the code, finds your session, and knows who you are.\n\nSo a cookie is like a **wristband at a festival**. Show it at each gate and you do not have to prove yourself again. The wristband itself is not you, but whoever wears it is treated as you, which is why stolen session cookies are valuable to attackers.\n\n![Logging in gives the browser a cookie that is sent with every later request so the server recognises it](local:cookie-session.svg)\n\n## Other places a site can remember things\n\n- **Local storage**: a larger bag of settings the site keeps in your browser (your language, a half-filled form).\n- **Tokens**: modern apps and APIs often use a **token** instead of a cookie: a code passed with each request in a header. The principle is the same. We cover tokens in the API module.\n\n## Why does this matter?\n\n- **Privacy law**: in the UK, cookies that are not strictly necessary (analytics, advertising) need the user\'s consent. That is why you see cookie banners.\n- **Security**: sessions should expire after inactivity, and logging out should destroy the session. A shared PC in a library or a tenant\'s home makes this matter.\n- **User experience**: a session that times out in 10 minutes while a contractor is filling in a long form on site is a bad design, whatever the security team\'s reasons.\n\n::: try\n**See your own cookies.**\n1. Go to any site you are logged into, such as your email or the council intranet.\n2. Press `F12`, open the **Application** tab (Chrome/Edge) or **Storage** tab (Firefox), and expand **Cookies** on the left.\n3. Click the site. You will see a table. Look for names like `session`, `sid` or `token`, and an **Expires** column showing how long it lasts.\n4. Now log out of the site and look again. The session cookie should be gone or replaced. If it is still there, ask yourself why.\n\n**Do not** share or paste the cookie values anywhere: they work like keys to your account.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nThink about timeouts and shared devices on this project. A technical officer using a shared tablet, a customer support adviser switching tasks, and a contractor in the rain with muddy gloves all have different needs from the same login system.\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
