INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'What is an API?', 'The waiter analogy, and why almost everything modern is built on APIs.', '## Start with a restaurant\n\nYou sit at a table. You cannot walk into the kitchen and cook, and the chef does not come out to take your order. Instead there is a **waiter**. You look at the **menu** (the list of what you are allowed to ask for), tell the waiter what you want, and they bring back the result. You never need to know how the kitchen works.\n\n![An API compared with a waiter between customer and kitchen](local:api-waiter.svg)\n\nThat is an **API**: **Application Programming Interface**. It is a defined way for one piece of software to ask another to do something or hand over some data, without needing to know how the other one works inside.\n\n- The **menu** is the API\'s documentation: the list of things you can ask for.\n- The **order** is a **request**.\n- The **food coming back** is the **response**.\n- The **kitchen** is the system that holds the data and does the work.\n\n## Why not just give access to the database?\n\nBecause it would be unsafe and chaotic. An API gives the owner of a system control: they decide exactly what can be asked, check who is asking, apply their rules every time and change the kitchen without any customer noticing, as long as the menu stays the same. This is why a supplier can upgrade their whole system and your integration keeps working.\n\n## APIs are everywhere\n\nWhen a weather widget shows the forecast on a website, an API supplied it. When you pay with a card, APIs link the shop, the card network and the bank. When "Sign in with Microsoft" appears, an API call confirms who you are. When your phone shows a map inside a food-delivery app, that is a mapping API at work. The modern internet is largely software talking to software, and APIs are the language.\n\n::: repairs\nIn a repairs service, APIs are how the housing system tells the contractor\'s app about a new job, how the contractor\'s app reports "completed" back, how the finance system gets the value to pay, and how the tenant portal shows live progress. If you hear a supplier say "we have a fully documented API", that is the menu, and it is worth reading it.\n:::\n\n::: try\nOpen `https://api.postcodes.io/postcodes/EH99 1SP` in your browser. (If your browser complains about the space, use `EH99%201SP`.) That is the Scottish Parliament\'s postcode. You will see a block of text with latitude, longitude, council area and more. You have just **called an API from your address bar**. A website could use the same call to fill in a tenant\'s address, fetch their council area, or check which contractor patch a postcode belongs to.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nAPI = a controlled doorway with a menu. One system asks. The other decides whether and how to answer.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
