INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Anatomy of a request and a response', 'The parts of every API conversation: URL, method, headers, body and what comes back.', '## Every API call has the same ingredients\n\nWhether it is a weather widget or a payment, an API request is built from the same few pieces. Learn them once and every API in the world becomes legible.\n\nA **request** contains:\n\n- **The method**: the verb, which says what you want to do: get, create, replace, update or delete. The next lesson covers these in detail.\n- **The URL**: the address, which says what you are talking about. The server part is the **base URL** (`https://api.example.gov.uk`), and the rest is the **endpoint** (`/repairs/4821`), the specific "menu item".\n- **Headers**: labels on the envelope. They say things like what format you are sending, what format you would like back, and who you are (for example, an authentication token).\n- **A body** (sometimes): the actual information you are sending, such as the details of a new repair. Reading data usually has no body. Creating or changing data does.\n\nA **response** contains:\n\n- **A status code**: a three-digit number saying how it went (the lesson after next).\n- **Headers**: labels about the answer, such as its format and how long it can be cached.\n- **A body** (usually): the data you asked for, normally in a format called JSON.\n\n## A real example, written out\n\nHere is what a request to fetch repair 4821 might look like on the wire:\n\n```\nGET /repairs/4821 HTTP/1.1\nHost: api.example-council.gov.uk\nAccept: application/json\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...\n```\n\nand the response:\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  "id": 4821,\n  "address": "12 Example Street, Edinburgh",\n  "problem": "Leaking kitchen tap",\n  "priority": "routine",\n  "status": "awaiting appointment"\n}\n```\n\nYou can read this! Method `GET`, path `/repairs/4821`, asking for JSON. Back comes `200 OK` and the repair\'s details.\n\n## Query strings: asking for a filtered list\n\nYou can add filters to a URL after a `?`, as `name=value` pairs joined with `&`:\n\n`/repairs?status=open&priority=emergency&limit=20`\n\nThis says "give me up to 20 open emergency repairs". These **query parameters** are how searching, filtering and paging work. If a supplier\'s API cannot filter, you may have to download everything and filter it yourself, which is slow and costly.\n\n::: try\n**See a real request.** Open `https://api.postcodes.io/postcodes/EH99%201SP` in a tab, press `F12`, open the **Network** tab, then reload. Click the first row.\n\n- Under **Headers**, find the **Request URL**, the **Request Method** (it will say `GET`) and the **Status Code** (200).\n- Look at **Request Headers**: find `Accept`. This is your browser telling the server what it can read.\n- Look at **Response Headers**: find `Content-Type`. It should say `application/json`.\n- Click **Response** to see the body.\n\nYou have now read both halves of a real API conversation.\n:::\n\n::: tip\nThe request/response pair is the same shape as in the browser lesson. A web page and an API call are the same technology: the difference is that a page returns HTML for people to read, while an API returns data (usually JSON) for software to read.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
