INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Status codes: what the server says back', 'Reading the number that tells you whether it worked, and whose fault it is if it did not.', '## Three digits, five families\n\nEvery API response starts with a **status code**. The first digit tells you the whole story:\n\n- **1xx**: information. Rarely seen.\n- **2xx**: **success**. It worked.\n- **3xx**: **redirection**. Look somewhere else.\n- **4xx**: **the client\'s fault**. The request was wrong somehow.\n- **5xx**: **the server\'s fault**. The request was fine; the server failed.\n\nThe 4xx versus 5xx difference is the most useful thing you can learn here, because it tells you instantly who needs to fix it.\n\n## The codes worth knowing\n\n**Success**\n- `200 OK`: it worked, and here is the result.\n- `201 Created`: it worked and a new thing now exists (the usual answer to a POST).\n- `204 No Content`: it worked and there is nothing to send back (common for DELETE).\n\n**Redirects**\n- `301 Moved Permanently` and `302 Found`: it lives somewhere else now. Your browser follows automatically.\n\n**Client errors (you got something wrong)**\n- `400 Bad Request`: the request was malformed or missing something.\n- `401 Unauthorized`: the server does not know who you are (not logged in, or token missing or expired).\n- `403 Forbidden`: the server knows who you are, but you are not allowed to do this.\n- `404 Not Found`: that address does not exist (or the thing is gone).\n- `409 Conflict`: the request clashes with the current state (someone else changed it first).\n- `422 Unprocessable Content`: the request was well-formed but fails the rules (for example a postcode that does not exist).\n- `429 Too Many Requests`: you have hit the **rate limit**. Slow down.\n\n**Server errors (they got something wrong)**\n- `500 Internal Server Error`: something broke inside the server. A bug or crash.\n- `502 Bad Gateway` / `504 Gateway Timeout`: the server in front could not get an answer from the one behind it.\n- `503 Service Unavailable`: temporarily unable to cope: overloaded or down for maintenance.\n\n## A way to remember 401 vs 403\n\nYou arrive at a building. **401**: the receptionist does not know who you are ("who are you?"). **403**: the receptionist knows exactly who you are, and you are still not allowed in that room.\n\n::: try\n**Provoke some status codes safely.** Open these in a browser tab, then check the status in the **Network** tab (`F12`):\n\n- `https://httpbin.org/status/200` (success)\n- `https://httpbin.org/status/404` (not found)\n- `https://httpbin.org/status/503` (service unavailable)\n- `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/9999` (a genuine 404: no such post)\n- `https://httpbin.org/status/418` (a joke code: "I am a teapot", from a real April Fools\' standard)\n\nWatch the page itself: it may look blank for some of these, but the status code tells you what the server meant.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nWhen an integration fails, always ask for the **status code**. "It\'s not working" is nothing. "It returned 403" tells you permissions need sorting. "It returned 500" means the supplier has a bug. "It returned 429" means you are calling too often. Each leads to a different person and a different fix.\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
