INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'JSON and other data formats', 'How data is written down for machines to read, and how to read it yourself.', '## Agreeing on a format\n\nFor two systems to exchange information they must agree on how to write it down, just as two people need a shared language. The most common format for APIs today is **JSON** (JavaScript Object Notation). Despite the name, it is not tied to JavaScript. It is simple, compact, and readable by both humans and machines.\n\n## Reading JSON\n\nJSON has only a few building blocks:\n\n- **Curly braces `{ }`** hold an **object**: a set of labelled values, like a record.\n- **Square brackets `[ ]`** hold a **list**: several of the same sort of thing.\n- Each value has a **name** in quotes, a colon, and the **value**: text in quotes, a number, `true`/`false`, or `null` (nothing).\n\nA list of two repairs:\n\n```\n{\n  "repairs": [\n    {\n      "id": 4821,\n      "address": "12 Example Street",\n      "emergency": false,\n      "tenant": { "name": "A. Tenant", "phone": "07700 900123" },\n      "photos": ["leak1.jpg", "leak2.jpg"]\n    },\n    {\n      "id": 4822,\n      "address": "3 Sample Road",\n      "emergency": true,\n      "tenant": null,\n      "photos": []\n    }\n  ],\n  "total": 2\n}\n```\n\nYou can read this without training. The first repair has an address, a flag saying it is not an emergency, a nested tenant record and two photos. The second is an emergency with no tenant on file and no photos. Notice that records can sit **inside** other records: that nesting is how JSON describes real-world relationships.\n\n## The alternatives\n\n- **XML**: older, wordier, uses tags like HTML (`<repair><id>4821</id></repair>`). Still common in older public sector and finance systems, and in "SOAP" APIs.\n- **CSV**: simple rows and columns, like a spreadsheet. Good for bulk exports and imports, poor at nesting.\n- **Files** such as PDF, images and spreadsheets, sent as attachments.\n\n## Why format matters to the Repairs project\n\n- When two systems use **different names for the same thing** (`postcode` versus `post_code` versus `PostalCode`), someone must write a **mapping**. Mapping is a big, hidden cost of integration.\n- Dates cause endless trouble. Is `03/04/2026` the third of April or the fourth of March? Good APIs use an unambiguous standard such as `2026-04-03` (year-month-day).\n- **Validity**: a missing comma or quote in JSON makes the whole message unreadable. Tools called validators and formatters help.\n\n::: try\n1. Open `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1`. That is real JSON from a free practice API. Find the nested objects (`address`, `company`) and the list-like parts.\n2. Open `https://api.postcodes.io/postcodes/EH99%201SP`. Find the `"result"` object and, inside it, the council area (`admin_district`) and `"country"`. Note how much is returned from one postcode.\n3. If your browser shows JSON as an unreadable block, use the "pretty print" option, or paste it into `https://jsonformatter.org` to see it laid out.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nSpend an hour with the supplier\'s API documentation and a sample repair as JSON. Walk through each field with a repairs expert: which are mandatory, which are optional, which are free text, which use a fixed list (codes, such as priority types). You will find gaps in understanding that no one has had time to notice.\n:::', 5, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
