INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Keeping APIs secure: keys, tokens and OAuth', 'How an API knows who is asking, and what to ask a supplier about securing the connection.', '## An open door is a problem\n\nIf an API simply answered anyone, anyone could read tenants\' details or create bogus repairs. So APIs insist on two things:\n\n- **Authentication**: *who are you?*\n- **Authorisation**: *are you allowed to do this?*\n\n(Remember the 401 and 403 status codes from earlier.)\n\n## Three common ways to prove who you are\n\n**API keys.** A long secret code issued to an app, like a membership number. The app sends it with every request. Simple, widely used, fine for low-risk data. The weakness: whoever has the key *is* that app, so it must never be shared, emailed or put in public code.\n\n**Tokens.** After a login, the system issues a **token**: a temporary digital pass that the app presents with each request, usually in the `Authorization` header (that "Bearer ..." line from earlier). Tokens expire after a short time, so a stolen one soon stops working. This is the same idea as the cookie in the browser lesson.\n\n**OAuth.** A standard way to let one app act for you in another system **without giving it your password**. You will have used it whenever you clicked "Sign in with Microsoft" or "Allow this app to access your calendar". You see a screen saying what the app wants permission to do; you approve; the app receives a limited token. You can revoke it later.\n\n## Scope: the principle of least privilege\n\nGood tokens are narrow. A contractor\'s app should be able to read *its own* jobs and update their status. It should not be able to read every tenant\'s phone number or delete repairs. That limit is called **scope**. When designing an integration, always ask "what is the least access this needs?"\n\n## Other protections\n\n- **Rate limiting**: capping how many calls any one caller can make, to stop overload or scraping.\n- **Encryption** (HTTPS) on every call, always.\n- **Logging and auditing**: a record of who called what and when, so problems and misuse can be traced.\n- **Secrets management**: keys and tokens stored in a secure vault, not pasted into documents, emails or chat.\n\n::: try\n**See OAuth in the wild.** Visit a website that offers "Sign in with Google" or "Sign in with Microsoft" and start the login (do not complete it if you do not want to). Read the permission screen carefully: it lists exactly what the app is asking to do. That list is the **scope**. Would you be comfortable approving it?\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nQuestions to ask any supplier about their API security: How do we authenticate? Can access be restricted to specific actions and records? How are keys rotated and revoked? Are all calls logged? What happens if a contractor\'s token is stolen? How is personal data protected in transit and at rest?\n:::\n\n::: remember\nNever paste an API key or token into an email, a chat, a Word document or a screenshot. Treat them like passwords.\n:::', 6, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
