INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'REST, webhooks and other API styles', 'The jargon you will hear in supplier meetings, explained, plus a short history of how APIs spread.', '## A short history of APIs\n\nAPIs in the sense of the word you hear today began to spread around 2000, when companies such as Salesforce and eBay started letting other software connect to their systems over the web. In the same year, Roy Fielding, one of the web\'s architects, described a style for designing web APIs in his doctoral thesis, which he called **REST**. Twitter, Facebook, Google and Amazon opened APIs through the 2000s, and the arrival of smartphones in 2007 and 2008 turned APIs into infrastructure: every app needs a back end to talk to.\n\n## REST\n\n**REST** (Representational State Transfer) is not a product but a **style** of design that has become the default. In a REST API:\n\n- Every thing (a repair, a tenant) has its own **URL**: `/repairs/4821`.\n- You use the standard **HTTP methods** (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) to work with it.\n- Each request is **self-contained**: it carries everything the server needs, including who you are.\n- Data usually comes back as **JSON**.\n\nIf a supplier says "we have a RESTful API", this is what they mean. It is by far the most common style, and the one you will practise in this course.\n\n## SOAP\n\nAn older style that wraps every message in XML, with a strict formal contract. Still found in older government, finance and housing systems. More rigid and heavier than REST but with built-in standards for security and reliability. If the landscape includes SOAP interfaces, expect more effort to connect them to modern tools.\n\n## GraphQL\n\nA newer style where the caller specifies **exactly which fields it wants** in one query, instead of getting a fixed response. Popular with big apps with many different screens. You may meet it, but REST remains far more common in public sector work.\n\n## Webhooks: "do not call us, we will call you"\n\nA normal API needs you to ask. If you want to know when a repair has been completed, you would have to keep asking "is it done yet? is it done yet?" (called **polling**), which wastes effort and is slow to notice.\n\nA **webhook** reverses it. You give the other system a web address of yours, and it **calls you** the moment something happens: "repair 4821 has just been completed". Webhooks are the neat way to keep systems in sync in near real time, and they are commonly used for notifications, payments and status changes.\n\n![Polling means repeatedly asking for news, a webhook means being told once when something happens](local:polling-vs-webhook.svg)\n\n## Events and queues\n\nFor bigger systems, changes are published as **events** ("repair completed") to a **message queue** or event stream, and any system that cares can pick them up in its own time. It is like a noticeboard rather than a phone call, and it makes systems less tightly dependent on each other.\n\n## API documentation and OpenAPI\n\nGood APIs come with documentation describing every endpoint, method and field, ideally in a machine-readable standard called **OpenAPI** (also known as Swagger), which can generate an interactive page where you can try calls. A supplier who cannot show this documentation, or says "you will need to talk to our developers", is a risk flag.\n\n::: try\nLook at some real, public API documentation:\n- `https://api.postcodes.io` (simple and friendly: click through the endpoints).\n- `https://petstore.swagger.io` is a demonstration of an OpenAPI page: expand an endpoint, click "Try it out", and execute a call in your browser.\n\nNotice how each endpoint lists the method, URL, parameters and example responses. That is what a good menu looks like.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nDo you need **real-time** updates (a webhook or events), or is "refresh every 15 minutes" good enough? Emergency repairs and live appointment tracking suggest the former. Monthly reporting suggests the latter. The answer drives cost.\n:::', 7, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
