INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Hands-on: calling an API yourself', 'Perform GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE with your own hands against a safe practice API, and watch how each behaves.', '## Time to get your hands dirty\n\nEverything in this module so far has been description. This lesson is practice. You will use a free practice API called **JSONPlaceholder** that exists purely for people to experiment on. It pretends to store things, but nothing you do is saved, so you **cannot break anything**.\n\nYou will use your browser\'s **Console**, where you can type small instructions. You do not need to understand the code; read the comment above each line, paste it and press Enter.\n\n## Setup\n\n1. Open `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com` in a browser tab. (Doing it here keeps the browser happy about security rules.)\n2. Press `F12` and open the **Console** tab. Also open the **Network** tab in the same panel if you want to watch the requests.\n3. Chrome and Edge may warn you about pasting. If so, type `allow pasting` and press Enter first.\n\nOn a managed council PC the console may be locked down. If so, try a personal device.\n\n::: try\n**1. GET: read a post.** Paste and press Enter:\n\n```\nfetch(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1\')\n  .then(r => r.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n```\n\nYou should see an object with `userId`, `id`, `title` and `body`. Click the little arrow to expand it. Think of the post as a repair record.\n:::\n\n::: try\n**2. POST: create a new "repair".**\n\n```\nfetch(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts\', {\n  method: \'POST\',\n  headers: { \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ title: \'Leaking kitchen tap\', body: \'Under the sink, getting worse\', userId: 1 })\n})\n  .then(r => { console.log(\'Status:\', r.status); return r.json() })\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n```\n\nLook at the **status** (it should be `201`, Created) and at the response. The server has given your new item an `id` (101). In a real system that is your repair reference number.\n:::\n\n::: try\n**3. PUT: replace the whole record.**\n\n```\nfetch(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1\', {\n  method: \'PUT\',\n  headers: { \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ id: 1, title: \'Replaced title\', body: \'Replaced body\', userId: 1 })\n})\n  .then(r => r.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n```\n\nYou sent every field and the whole record was replaced.\n:::\n\n::: try\n**4. PATCH: change one field only.**\n\n```\nfetch(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1\', {\n  method: \'PATCH\',\n  headers: { \'Content-Type\': \'application/json\' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ title: \'Only the title changed\' })\n})\n  .then(r => r.json())\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\n```\n\nCompare this with the PUT result. With PATCH you sent only `title`, and the response still shows the **original body and userId**. That is the PUT-versus-PATCH difference, demonstrated.\n:::\n\n::: try\n**5. DELETE: remove it.**\n\n```\nfetch(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1\', { method: \'DELETE\' })\n  .then(r => console.log(\'Status:\', r.status))\n```\n\nYou should see status `200`. Now run the GET from step 1 again: the post is still there, because this practice API does not really delete anything. Real systems do, which is why DELETE deserves care and often becomes "cancel" or "archive" instead.\n:::\n\n::: try\n**6. Provoke an error on purpose.**\n\n```\nfetch(\'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/99999\')\n  .then(r => console.log(\'Status:\', r.status))\n```\n\nA `404`. Try a typo such as `/postz/1` too. You are now reading status codes like a developer.\n:::\n\n## What you have just learned\n\nYou made five of the HTTP methods work, saw the status codes, watched JSON go in and out, and found out first-hand why PUT and PATCH differ. That is the substance of an API course for most people. Everything else is detail on top.\n\n::: repairs\nNow translate. Replace "post" with "repair". `POST /repairs` raises a new job. `PATCH /repairs/4821` updates just the status. `GET /repairs/4821` shows it. `DELETE /repairs/4821` cancels it. The ability to say this clearly to a supplier, and to read their documentation in the same terms, is the skill this module exists to build.\n:::', 8, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
