INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Front end and back end', 'The two halves of every application and who does what.', '## Two halves of one service\n\nAlmost every application has two halves that cooperate.\n\nThe **front end** is everything the user sees and touches: layout, colours, buttons, forms, menus. It runs **on the user\'s own device**, in their browser or on their phone, and is built with HTML, CSS and JavaScript (or, for phone apps, the phone\'s own languages).\n\nThe **back end** is everything behind the scenes: the **business rules** (what counts as an emergency, who can approve a cost), the **processing** (calculating a price from the Schedule of Rates), the connection to the **database**, the **security**, and the **integrations** with other systems. It runs on **servers**.\n\nThe front end and back end talk to each other through an **API**: exactly the thing you have just learned to read.\n\n## The restaurant again\n\nIn the waiter analogy, the front end is the **dining room**: tables, menu, atmosphere. The back end is the **kitchen** and the stock room. Customers judge the meal by the dining room, but it is the kitchen that decides whether the food is any good, and whether it arrives at all.\n\n## Why the split is good\n\n- **Specialist teams** can work in parallel: designers and front-end developers on the experience, back-end developers on rules and data.\n- **One back end can serve many front ends**: the same repairs back end can power the tenant portal, the contractor app, the customer support screen and a future chatbot, all through the same API.\n- **Change is easier**: you can refresh the look of a service without touching the rules, or change a rule once for every channel.\n\n## Where do rules belong?\n\nAn important design question: where is a business rule enforced? If "emergency repairs must be attended within 4 hours" is only checked on the screen (front end), anyone who talks to the API directly can bypass it. Important rules must be enforced in the **back end**, with the front end giving friendly early feedback. When a supplier says "that validation is in the app", ask whether it is also checked on the server.\n\n::: try\nOpen a repair-reporting form on any website, such as a council\'s. Fill in a field wrongly (an invalid email address, say) and watch what happens. Some forms complain instantly (front-end check). Others only complain after pressing Send (back-end check, with a round trip to the server). Most good ones do both. Notice which you can spot, and use the **Network** tab: no request means the front end handled it alone.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nWho owns the rules about repair priorities and response times? Are they written down clearly enough that a developer can turn them into back-end logic? Often the biggest barrier to a good system is not technology but undocumented rules living in people\'s heads. This is exactly where repairs expertise is irreplaceable.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
