INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Databases: where the facts live', 'Tables, rows, columns, relationships, and a gentle first look at SQL.', '## A very organised filing cabinet\n\nA **database** is an organised store of information that software can search and update quickly and safely, even with many users at once. The software that runs a database, stores the data safely and answers requests is called a **database management system (DBMS)**; MySQL, PostgreSQL and Microsoft SQL Server are well-known examples, and in everyday talk people often just say "the database" for both. The most common kind is a **relational database**, which keeps data in **tables**, like spreadsheets that can be linked together.\n\nThink of a table of repairs:\n\n- Each **row** is one record (one repair).\n- Each **column** is one piece of information (reference, address, priority, status).\n- Each row has a unique **ID**, so it can be referred to unambiguously. This is called the **primary key**.\n\n![Anatomy of a database table: columns, rows, primary key, foreign key and cells](local:table-anatomy.svg)\n\n## Why several tables?\n\nYou could put everything in one huge sheet, but you would repeat the contractor\'s name and phone number on every job they ever did. Instead, relational databases keep a **contractors** table and a **repairs** table and link them: each repair holds the **ID of its contractor** instead of copying the details. That link is called a **relationship**, and the column that holds it is a **foreign key**: a value that matches the primary key of a row in another table.\n\nBenefits: change the contractor\'s phone number **once** and every repair sees it. No duplicates, no inconsistency. This idea is called **normalisation** and it is one reason databases beat spreadsheets for serious work.\n\n## The schema: the blueprint\n\nThe **schema** is the blueprint of a database: which tables exist, which columns each one has, what type of value goes in each (text, number, date), and how the tables link. Designing it is a bit like designing a form: decide what you need to know before anyone starts filling it in. Changing a schema later is possible but can be slow and risky once millions of records exist, which is why it deserves careful thought up front.\n\n![A database schema: four linked tables for properties, tenants, repairs and contractors](local:schema-relationships.svg)\n\nRead the diagram as a map. Each box is a table. **KEY** marks the unique label for a row. **LINK** marks a column that holds the key of a row in another table, and the lines show those links. "1 : many" means one row on one side can be linked to many rows on the other: one property can have many repairs, and one contractor can do many repairs.\n\n## SQL: asking the database questions\n\nDatabases are queried with **SQL** (Structured Query Language, often said "sequel"). You do not need to write it, but it is remarkably readable:\n\n```\nSELECT reference, address, priority\nFROM repairs\nWHERE status = \'open\' AND priority = \'emergency\'\nORDER BY created_at;\n```\n\nIn plain English: "Show me the reference, address and priority of every open emergency repair, oldest first." That one statement can search a million rows in a blink.\n\n![A database query scanning rows and returning only those that match the conditions](local:sql-query.svg)\n\nWatch the animation: the database software checks each row against the conditions and returns only the rows that pass both. The data itself just sits in tables; it is the **database management system** (the software running the database, such as MySQL, PostgreSQL or SQL Server) that reads your SQL, plans the quickest way to find the rows and does the work. You describe **what** you want, and it works out **how** to find it.\n\nThe four verbs you will most often see in SQL map exactly onto the API methods you met: `SELECT` is read (GET), `INSERT` is create (POST), `UPDATE` is change (PUT/PATCH), `DELETE` is remove (DELETE).\n\n## Why data structure matters so much\n\nThe structure of the database, its **data model**, quietly shapes everything the system can do. If a system stores an address as one free-text line, you cannot easily analyse repairs by street or postcode. If it records "who changed the status and when", you can audit it. If it does not, you cannot. Good data modelling at the start saves enormous pain later, and it is a domain question as much as a technical one: what do you need to know about a repair?\n\n## Other kinds of storage\n\n- **Document databases**: store whole records as flexible JSON-like documents. Good when records vary a lot.\n- **Data warehouses**: large, separate stores built for **reporting and analysis** across many systems, so that heavy reports do not slow down the live system.\n- **Files and object storage**: where photos, PDFs and documents go.\n\n::: try\nYou can practise SQL with no installation at all. Visit `https://www.w3schools.com/sql/trysql.asp?filename=trysql_select_all` and press **Run SQL**. You are querying a sample database of customers. Then change the query to `SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE Country = \'UK\';` and run it again. You have just filtered a table.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nSketch the **repairs** table you would want on a single sheet of paper: what columns? Which ones link to other tables (properties, tenants, contractors, SOR codes)? This simple exercise often exposes disagreements about what a "repair" or a "job" actually is, which are far cheaper to resolve on paper than in software.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
