INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Architecture: monoliths, microservices and legacy systems', 'Why old systems are hard to change, what "technical debt" means and why this project is not building from a blank sheet.', '## How big pieces of software are organised\n\n**Architecture** is the high-level plan of how a system is organised into parts and how those parts connect: the blueprint rather than the bricks.\n\n**Monolith.** One large application where everything (screens, rules, data access, integrations) is built and released together as a single unit. Simpler to start with and often perfectly fine, but changing one thing means retesting and re-releasing the whole thing, and a fault in one area can bring down the rest.\n\n**Microservices.** The system is split into many small, independent services (for example: appointments, payments, notifications, tenant records), each doing one job and talking to the others through APIs. Easier to change and scale one part, but much more complex to run and monitor. It is a trade-off, not an automatic upgrade.\n\nMost real organisations have a mixture, and "microservices" is not a prize you should demand of a supplier. Ask instead: **can this system change quickly and safely, and does it connect to others cleanly?**\n\n## Legacy systems\n\nA **legacy system** is an older system still in use, usually because it is critical and replacing it is risky and expensive. Many council housing, finance and revenues systems are legacy: some date back decades, running on older technology with few people left who understand them.\n\nLegacy systems are hard because:\n\n- They may have **no API** at all, or only an outdated one.\n- Their **rules and data** are poorly documented; the original builders have left.\n- Everything depends on them, so any change feels dangerous.\n- They may be tied to a single supplier or an obsolete platform.\n\nCommon ways to deal with them: **wrap** them with a modern API layer so other systems can connect, **replace** them gradually piece by piece (sometimes called the "strangler" approach, where new parts take over old functions one at a time), or do a **full replacement** when the case is strong. Each has costs and risks, and part of this project\'s job is helping the organisation choose well.\n\n## Technical debt\n\n**Technical debt** is the idea that quick fixes, shortcuts and ageing technology are like borrowing: they save time now but you pay interest later in slower change, more bugs and higher risk. Like financial debt, some is sensible, and it should be **visible and managed**, not hidden. When a supplier says "that will be easy" about something in a legacy system, it is worth asking what makes it easy, and what is being left alone.\n\n## Integration patterns\n\nHow do systems get connected? Three broad ways:\n\n- **Point-to-point**: each pair is wired directly. Quick for two systems, a tangle for ten.\n- **Middleware / integration platform**: a hub that all systems connect to, which translates and routes between them. Easier to manage as things grow.\n- **Data warehouse or shared data layer**: systems feed a common store that others read for reporting and analysis.\n\n![Repairs systems connected through APIs](local:repairs-ecosystem.svg)\n\n::: repairs\nMap the real landscape. List every system a repair touches from report to payment and draw the arrows. Which ones have APIs? Which pass files overnight? Which are re-keyed by hand? That map is the most useful document this project can produce, and you do not need to be technical to draw it.\n:::\n\n::: try\nAsk a colleague in IT, finance or housing: "What is the oldest system we still depend on, and who understands it?" Their answer, and the pause before it, will teach you more about legacy than any article.\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
