INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'How software is built, tested and released', 'Environments, version control, automated pipelines and why your testing expertise is so valuable.', '## Software is made in stages, in separate copies\n\nProfessional software is never edited directly on the live system that people are using. Instead it moves through separate copies, called **environments**:\n\n![Development, test, UAT and live environments](local:environments.svg)\n\n- **Development**: where developers build and break things freely.\n- **Test**: where testers check that it works as designed.\n- **UAT (User Acceptance Testing)**: where the people who will actually use it, **staff and subject experts**, check it does what the job really needs.\n- **Live (production)**: the real thing, used by real tenants, staff and contractors.\n\nEach stage should be as similar to live as possible, with realistic (but never real, unprotected personal) data.\n\n## Version control: the time machine\n\nDevelopers store code in a **version control** system, nearly always **Git**. It records every change, who made it and why, so that work can be compared, combined from many people, and undone. Services such as GitHub and Azure DevOps host this. For you, the important thing is that a supplier who does **not** use version control is a serious red flag.\n\n## Automated pipelines: CI/CD\n\n**CI/CD** stands for Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery. It is an automated pipeline that, each time a developer makes a change, **builds** the software, runs automatic **tests**, and (if they pass) moves it towards release. This makes releases small, frequent and safer, instead of huge, rare and terrifying "big bang" events.\n\n## Kinds of testing\n\n- **Unit tests**: tiny automatic checks of single pieces of code.\n- **Integration tests**: do the pieces, and the connected systems, work together?\n- **Regression tests**: did this change break something that used to work?\n- **Performance tests**: does it cope with many users at once?\n- **Security tests**: can it be broken into?\n- **User acceptance testing (UAT)**: does it support the real job?\n\n## Where your expertise matters most\n\nDevelopers can tell whether software does what the **specification** says. Only people who know the work can tell whether the **specification** is right. A repairs expert will spot "that is not how a gas safety emergency works" or "we never close a job before the post-inspection". UAT done well, with realistic scenarios and honest feedback, is one of the highest-value things a non-technical team can contribute.\n\n## Releases and change\n\n- A **release** is a version of the software going live. Good teams release often, with a way to **roll back** if something breaks.\n- A **release note** tells users what changed.\n- **Downtime** windows, **feature flags** (switching features on for some users first) and **phased rollouts** reduce risk.\n\n::: try\nLook at the changelog of an app on your phone: open the app store listing and read "What\'s new". That is a release note. Notice how often a good app updates, and how small each change is. Then ask: how often does our repairs system release changes, and how would we know?\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nWrite three realistic UAT scenarios from the repairs world. For example: "A tenant reports a no-heating call on a Friday evening in winter, with a vulnerable resident in the household. Walk it through from report to completion." These are test cases a developer could never invent, and they will find problems that no script would.\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
