INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'User-centred design and the phases of delivery', 'Discovery, alpha, beta and live, and why good services start by understanding people, not by building.', '## Start with the person, not the software\n\nThe most important idea in modern public-sector digital work is also the simplest: **start with user needs**. Not "what system do we want?" but "what is the person trying to do, and what gets in their way?" That person might be a tenant with a leaking roof, a contractor trying to close a job, or a customer support adviser with the system slowing them down.\n\nThis approach is called **user-centred design**, and it is built into the **Digital Scotland Service Standard**, which public bodies in Scotland are expected to follow when building digital services, and which draws on the UK\'s GOV.UK approach.\n\n## The phases\n\nGood services are not built in one go from a long specification. They go through short phases, each ending with a decision.\n\n![Discovery, alpha, beta and live phases](local:delivery-phases.svg)\n\n- **Discovery**: learn about the problem and the people. Observe, interview, map the current process, find what actually causes failure demand (people ringing because something went wrong earlier). Output: a clear understanding and a recommendation about whether and what to build.\n- **Alpha**: try ideas cheaply. Build rough **prototypes**, test them with real users, throw away what does not work. Output: a proven approach, and evidence.\n- **Beta**: build it for real, release to a limited group, learn from actual use, fix, improve, expand. Includes security, accessibility and performance checks.\n- **Live**: it is open to everyone and continues to be improved. A live service is never "finished": it has a team to keep it working and responsive to change.\n\nAt each stage-gate ask: *have we learned enough to justify the next step, or should we stop or change direction?* Stopping early because you found out an idea does not work is **success**, not failure. It is far cheaper to learn in alpha than after a £2 million build.\n\n## Agile ways of working\n\nDelivery in these phases generally uses **agile** methods: small, cross-functional teams working in short cycles (often called sprints), showing working results often, and adapting to what they learn. If you have already worked through the Agile lessons on this site you will recognise the ideas: they are the same philosophy, applied to digital service delivery.\n\n## A cross-functional team\n\nA typical digital team brings together roles that you will hear mentioned:\n\n- **Service owner / product owner**: accountable for the service and its priorities.\n- **Delivery manager**: keeps the team working well and removes blockers.\n- **User researcher**: finds out what users really need and how they behave.\n- **Designers** (service, interaction, content): shape the experience.\n- **Developers and testers**: build and check it.\n- **Business analyst / subject matter experts**: bring deep knowledge of the process and rules. This is where subject experts such as repairs and council-governance specialists fit.\n\n::: try\nOpen `https://www.gov.uk/service-manual` and browse **Agile delivery** and **Service standard**. These are public, readable and full of real examples. Notice how many pages are about *people* and *learning*, not about technology.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nWrite down the single biggest source of avoidable contact in the current repairs process. (For example: tenants ringing to ask "when is my appointment?" because they get no confirmation.) That is a **failure demand** problem, and fixing it may be worth more than any new feature.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
