INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'UX, UI, wireframes, prototypes and design systems', 'The vocabulary of design, and how designers turn understanding into something you can see and test.', '## UX and UI are not the same\n\n- **UX (User Experience)** is the whole experience of getting something done: how easy, clear, quick and pleasant it is from start to finish, including the bits off-screen such as getting a text message at the right time.\n- **UI (User Interface)** is the visible surface: the screens, buttons, colours and text.\n\nA beautiful UI can sit on top of terrible UX: gorgeous screens that make you do the same data entry three times. A plain UI with excellent UX just works. The aim is the second.\n\n## From idea to something testable\n\nDesigners work through stages of increasing detail, learning at each:\n\n- **Sketches**: pen and paper, quick and disposable.\n- **Wireframes**: simple grey layouts showing *what goes where* with no colour or polish. Their purpose is to agree structure and content without arguing about fonts.\n- **Prototypes**: clickable mock-ups that feel real enough to test with users but have no working back end behind them. Tools include Figma, which is widely used and allows teams to comment live.\n- **Working software**: built only once the prototype has shown that it works for real people.\n\nShowing someone a prototype and watching them try to use it, saying nothing, is the cheapest and most valuable learning technique in design. Five users often uncover most of the big problems.\n\n## Design systems: do not reinvent the button\n\nA **design system** is a shared library of tested building blocks (buttons, forms, error messages, page layouts) and the rules for using them. It means services look and behave consistently, and teams do not waste time redesigning things that have already been done well and tested for accessibility.\n\nThe best-known public sector example is the **GOV.UK Design System**, used across UK government and widely adopted by councils. Its patterns for things such as "ask for an address", "ask for a date" or "show a confirmation page" are based on years of testing with real users.\n\n## Content design\n\nWords are part of the interface. **Content design** means writing in clear, plain language, using the terms users use (not internal jargon like "SOR code" or "DLO" on a screen meant for tenants), and putting the most important thing first. A tenant who does not understand "Your job has been raised and is awaiting scheduling" will pick up the phone.\n\n::: try\n**Explore a design system.** Visit `https://design-system.service.gov.uk/components/` and click through a few components such as **Button**, **Error message** and **Date input**. Each has examples, guidance on when to use it and when not to, and accessibility notes. Then open a few council or supplier websites and see whether they use similar patterns.\n\n**Then try a 10-minute paper test.** Draw a mobile screen for "report a repair" on a piece of paper. Ask a colleague (not on this project) to "report a leaking tap" using it, without help. Where do they hesitate? That hesitation is a design finding.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nLook at a recent screen of the repairs system. Which words would a tenant not understand? Which fields are asked for that the system could already know? Every unnecessary question loses people and costs money.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
