INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Accessibility: designing for everyone', 'The law, the standard, and quick checks anyone can do.', '## Who is affected?\n\nRoughly one in four people in the UK has a disability or long-term condition, and many more have **temporary** or **situational** limits: a broken arm, a bright sun on a phone screen, a noisy van, a first language that is not English. Housing tenants, as a group, include a higher-than-average proportion of people with disabilities, older people and people with low digital confidence. **Accessibility is not a niche concern for repairs services. It is the core audience.**\n\n## The law and the standard\n\nUK public sector bodies, including councils in Scotland, must meet the **Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) (No. 2) Accessibility Regulations 2018**. These require websites and apps to meet **WCAG level AA** (the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines; monitoring is now carried out against version 2.2) and to publish an **accessibility statement** explaining any parts that do not. The **Equality Act 2010** adds a wider duty not to discriminate, and the Public Sector Equality Duty requires public bodies to think ahead about the effect of what they do.\n\nWCAG is organised around four principles, summarised as **POUR**:\n\n- **Perceivable**: can people see, hear or otherwise take in the content? (alt text for images, captions, enough colour contrast.)\n- **Operable**: can people use it with a keyboard only, a switch, voice control or a screen reader?\n- **Understandable**: is the language clear and are errors explained?\n- **Robust**: does it work with different browsers and assistive technology?\n\n## Assistive technology\n\nPeople use all sorts of tools: **screen readers** that read pages aloud, **screen magnifiers**, **voice control**, **switch devices**, and **keyboard-only** navigation. These tools depend on well-built HTML, which is why a page can look perfect and still be unusable.\n\n## Common failures\n\n- **Low contrast text** (pale grey on white).\n- **Images without text descriptions**.\n- **Forms with unclear labels** or errors shown only in colour.\n- **Controls that cannot be reached by keyboard**.\n- **Time limits** that logout people who need longer.\n- **PDF forms** that cannot be read by screen readers.\n- **Jargon and long, complicated sentences**.\n\n## Also: digital inclusion\n\nNot everyone is online or confident. Good services keep a **non-digital route** (phone, face-to-face), design the digital route to be so good that most people choose it, and support those who need help. "Digital by default" must never become "digital only".\n\n::: try\n**Three five-minute checks you can do now on any page, including a local council\'s:**\n\n1. **Keyboard test.** Put your mouse aside. Press `Tab` repeatedly. Can you reach every link and button? Can you see where you are (a visible outline)? Can you press `Enter` to use them?\n2. **Zoom test.** Press `Ctrl` and `+` until the page is at 200%. Does it still work without sideways scrolling and without text overlapping?\n3. **Contrast test.** Use the free tool at `https://webaim.org/resources/contrastchecker/` with a text colour and background colour from the page. Does it pass AA?\n\nFor the full picture, try the free **WAVE** tool at `https://wave.webaim.org` and paste in a web address.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nThink about three tenants: one blind, one with limited English, one with a tremor that makes tapping small buttons hard. Walk through reporting a repair as each. What would stop them? What would you change first?\n:::', 3, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
