INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Service design: the whole journey, not just the screens', 'Mapping the end-to-end repair experience, including everything that happens off-screen.', '## A screen is the tip of the iceberg\n\nA tenant does not experience "the repairs portal". They experience **getting their leak fixed**: noticing it, finding how to report it, describing it, waiting, being told when someone is coming, letting someone in, the repair itself, a follow-up, and perhaps a complaint if any step goes wrong. A beautiful reporting screen is useless if the contractor never turns up.\n\n**Service design** is the practice of designing that **whole journey**, including the **front stage** (what the user sees) and the **back stage** (the people, processes, systems and policies that make it work).\n\n## Journey maps\n\nA **user journey map** lays out the steps a person goes through, across time, with what they **do**, **think** and **feel** at each. It shows the **pain points** (where it goes wrong) and **moments that matter** (where a small improvement has a big effect).\n\nA fragment for a repair journey might look like this:\n\n1. **Notices a leak.** Feels: worried. Needs: to know if it is urgent.\n2. **Looks for how to report it.** Feels: frustrated if the website hides it. Needs: a clear route.\n3. **Reports it.** Feels: unsure. Needs: a reference number and confirmation straight away.\n4. **Waits.** Feels: anxious. Needs: to know what happens next and when.\n5. **Appointment.** Needs: a slot that fits, reminders, a name and photo of who is coming.\n6. **Repair done.** Needs: to confirm it is fixed, and a way to say if it is not.\n\n## Service blueprints\n\nA **service blueprint** adds layers beneath the journey: the **front-stage** actions visible to the user, the **back-stage** actions by staff and contractors, and the **support systems** (software, data, policies) behind them. It reveals handovers, where information must pass from one team or system to another, and handovers are exactly where things go wrong and where APIs and integration matter.\n\n## Failure demand and the cost of delay\n\n**Failure demand** is contact caused by something not working earlier: "Where is my repair?", "I did not get a confirmation", "The contractor did not come". It is expensive, frustrating for everyone, and often **invisible in the numbers** because it is counted as normal workload. Reducing failure demand is often the strongest business case for transformation.\n\n## The people behind the screen\n\nA service also has **staff users**: customer support advisers, planners, technical officers, finance staff. A system that is lovely for tenants but miserable for the people who run it will fail in practice. Good service design includes them, too.\n\n## Measuring success\n\nA service needs measures that capture real outcomes, not just activity: **percentage of repairs fixed first visit**, **time to complete**, **tenant satisfaction**, **cost per repair**, **digital take-up**, **avoidable contact**. Choose them early, and make sure the system can report them.\n\n::: try\n**Map one real repair journey** on a large sheet of paper or a whiteboard. Down the left, write the user\'s steps. Beneath each, add rows for: what the tenant feels, what staff or contractors do behind the scenes, and which system holds the information at that moment. Circle every **handover**. Put a star beside every step where information is re-typed or someone has to phone to find out. You have just built the starting point of a service blueprint.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nThis is where subject experts\' knowledge is most powerful. A repairs expert knows what really happens on a repair; a finance and governance expert knows how money, rules and governance shape it. A blueprint drawn by both together will show gaps that neither would see alone, and gives the digital team the shared map it needs.\n:::\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Digital Scotland Service Standard](https://www.gov.scot/publications/digital-scotland-service-standard/)', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
