INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Personal data and UK GDPR', 'What the law says, the key principles, and what a DPIA is and when you need one.', '## Repairs data is personal data\n\nA repair record names a person, shows where they live, may mention health conditions (a vulnerable resident), family composition, when the house is empty, or a complaint history. That is **personal data**, and some of it is **special category** data (such as health) which needs even more protection.\n\nIn the UK the rules are in the **UK GDPR** and the **Data Protection Act 2018**, regulated by the **Information Commissioner\'s Office (ICO)**. Councils are subject to them like any other organisation, plus public sector duties.\n\n## The seven principles\n\nPlainly put, personal data must be:\n\n1. Used **lawfully, fairly and transparently**: with a legal basis and people told what happens.\n2. Used only for **specified, legitimate purposes**.\n3. **Minimised**: only what is needed.\n4. **Accurate** and kept up to date.\n5. **Kept no longer than necessary**: with a retention schedule.\n6. Protected with appropriate **security**.\n7. Handled **accountably**: you must be able to show you comply.\n\n## Legal bases\n\nYou need a lawful basis for using personal data. For councils the main ones are **public task** (delivering its statutory functions), **legal obligation**, and **contract** (for example the tenancy). **Consent** is used less often by public bodies because of the imbalance of power between the council and the person.\n\n## People\'s rights\n\nPeople can ask what data you hold (a **subject access request**), have inaccurate data corrected, in some cases have it erased, object to some uses, and have a human review automated decisions that significantly affect them. Systems must be built so that these requests can actually be answered. It must be possible to find all of one person\'s data across connected systems.\n\n## DPIA: the checkpoint for new systems\n\nA **Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA)** is a structured assessment, required before starting processing that is likely to be high risk, which asks: what data, why, where does it flow, who sees it, what could go wrong, and how do we reduce those risks? New systems that involve personal data, new data sharing between organisations, large-scale use of vulnerable people\'s data and certain uses of new technology (including AI) will usually need one. Involve the Data Protection Officer **early**, not at the end.\n\n## Data sharing and suppliers\n\nWhen a supplier handles personal data for you, they are usually a **processor** acting on the council\\\'s instructions, and the council is the **controller** responsible for how it is used. A written contract with specific data protection terms is required. Questions to ask include where the data is stored (UK, EU, elsewhere), who can access it, how long it is kept, how it is deleted when a contract ends, and what happens if there is a breach. Serious breaches must be reported to the ICO within 72 hours.\n\n## Data flows through APIs\n\nEvery API integration is a **data flow**. The principle of **data minimisation** applies: a contractor\'s app should receive what the contractor needs for the job (address, access details, nature of the work), not the tenant\'s whole history. Designing what flows through each API is as much a privacy decision as a technical one.\n\n::: try\nVisit the ICO\'s guide at `https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/` and find the page on DPIAs. Skim the screening questions. Then, with a repairs colleague, answer them for a feature in this project, for example: "contractors can see vulnerability flags for tenants". What would you decide?\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nConsider vulnerability information. It helps contractors be safe and sensitive, but it is special category data. What is the minimum a contractor needs to know? ("Needs a phone call before arrival" is enough without a diagnosis.) Designing the flow with this in mind is exactly the sort of judgement project colleagues are well placed to make.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nThis course gives general understanding, not legal advice. For decisions about this project, involve the Data Protection Officer and information governance team.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
