INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'AI, automation and the repairs service', 'What AI and automation can really do for a repairs service, where they go wrong and how to ask the right questions.', '## Three things often lumped together\n\n- **Automation**: software that follows fixed rules to do routine work without a person: "if a repair is marked complete and the invoice matches the rate, approve payment". Predictable, reliable, and has existed for decades.\n- **RPA (Robotic Process Automation)**: "software robots" that copy what a person does on screen, for example copying data from one system to another that has no API. Useful as a bridge, but fragile: if the screen changes, the robot breaks. Think of it as a temporary patch rather than a design.\n- **AI (artificial intelligence)**: systems that learn patterns from data rather than following only rules written by people. This includes **machine learning** (predicting, classifying) and **generative AI**, which produces text, images or code in response to a prompt. Large language models such as ChatGPT or Claude are generative AI.\n\n## Where AI might genuinely help in repairs\n\n- **Triage from a photo or description**: suggesting what the problem is and how urgent, for a person to confirm.\n- **Understanding free text**: classifying the thousands of messages and complaints received.\n- **Predicting**: which properties are at risk of damp and mould, which boilers are likely to fail, which jobs will overrun.\n- **Scheduling and routing**: working out the best order of jobs for each operative.\n- **Drafting and summarising**: letters, case notes, minutes. A human reviews the result.\n- **Chat and voice assistants**: answering simple questions, 24 hours a day, and passing to a person when needed.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n\n- **It can be confidently wrong.** Generative AI can produce fluent, plausible and false statements ("hallucinations"). Never rely on it for facts without checking.\n- **Bias.** If the data it learned from reflected unfair patterns, it can repeat or amplify them. In housing this can mean certain groups being deprioritised without anyone intending it.\n- **Opacity.** It can be hard to explain *why* a model decided something, which matters when a decision affects a person\'s home and safety.\n- **Privacy.** Putting personal data into a public AI tool may be a data breach. Know what the organisation permits.\n- **Over-trust.** A smooth chatbot can hide the fact that nobody is actually fixing the problem.\n\n## Principles for using AI in public services\n\n- Keep a **human in the loop** for decisions that significantly affect people, especially around safety and vulnerability.\n- Be **transparent**: tell people when they are dealing with AI and how to reach a person.\n- Complete a **DPIA** and consider an **equality impact assessment**.\n- **Test with real data and real scenarios** (the repairs team\'s own scenarios!) and keep monitoring after launch.\n- Be able to **explain** how a decision was reached and to correct errors.\n- Start with a **real problem**, not a fashionable tool.\n\nThe Scottish Government\'s AI Strategy ("Scotland\'s AI Strategy") sets out an aim for AI that is **trustworthy, ethical and inclusive**, and the **Scottish AI Alliance** provides guidance and resources. The UK government and the ICO also publish guidance on using AI with personal data.\n\n## Automation needs good foundations\n\nAutomation and AI amplify what exists. A good, clean, well-integrated process gets faster. A messy one gets messy faster. This is why data quality and good APIs come first: they are what let you automate safely later.\n\n::: try\n**Experience both sides.** If the organisation permits it, use an approved AI assistant with **no real personal data**, and ask it to "write a polite letter to a tenant explaining that a repair appointment has moved". Notice how good it sounds. Now ask it a factual question about a specific council policy it cannot possibly know. See whether it admits it does not know or invents an answer. Both outcomes teach you something.\n\nIf AI tools are not approved for you, read the NCSC\'s short guidance on AI at `https://www.ncsc.gov.uk` (search "AI") instead.\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nPick one repetitive task in the repairs process, such as re-keying invoice details or chasing appointment confirmations. Ask: is this a **rule** problem (automate it), a **missing-integration** problem (fix the API), or a **judgement** problem (maybe AI helps a human)? Getting this classification right avoids buying the wrong thing.\n:::', 4, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
