INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Talking to suppliers: the vocabulary and the questions', 'SLAs, uptime, roadmaps, lock-in and the sharp questions that separate real answers from sales talk.', '## You can now ask better questions than most buyers\n\nProcurement conversations with technology suppliers are full of confident jargon. You now know enough to see through most of it. This lesson turns this new knowledge into a practical toolkit.\n\n## Terms you will hear\n\n**SLA (Service Level Agreement).** The measurable promises in the contract: availability, response times, resolution times, and what happens (service credits, termination rights) if they are missed. Always ask: *how is it measured, who measures it, and what is the remedy?*\n\n**Uptime.** The percentage of time a service is working. It sounds high and hides big differences:\n\n- 99% allows about **3.7 days** of downtime a year.\n- 99.9% allows about **8.8 hours**.\n- 99.99% allows about **53 minutes**.\n\nAsk whether planned maintenance counts, and whether the figure refers to the whole service or just the servers.\n\n**RPO and RTO.** After a disaster, **RPO (Recovery Point Objective)** is how much data you could lose (the last 15 minutes? the last day?), and **RTO (Recovery Time Objective)** is how long until the service is back. Both should be stated, and **tested**.\n\n**Roadmap.** The supplier\'s plan of what they intend to build. It is intentions, not promises. Ask for **what is delivered** rather than what is planned, and check whether any feature you rely on is "on the roadmap".\n\n**Customisation versus configuration.** *Configuration* means changing the product through its own settings (low risk, upgrades safely). *Customisation* means changing the product\'s code or building special versions for you (costly, and often makes upgrades hard). A supplier that offers to "customise anything" may be building you a future legacy system.\n\n**Vendor lock-in.** Being so tied to one supplier\'s product or data format that leaving is prohibitively hard. Protect yourself with a clear **exit plan**: can you export all the data in a standard, usable form? What does it cost? How long does it take?\n\n**Multi-tenant versus single-tenant.** In a multi-tenant SaaS, many customers share one system; upgrades arrive for everyone together. In single-tenant, you have your own copy. Each has trade-offs for cost, control and flexibility.\n\n**Total cost of ownership (TCO).** Licence fees are only part of it: add implementation, integration, training, support, upgrades, data migration, and eventually exit.\n\n## The questions that matter\n\nOn **APIs and integration**\n- Is the API documented publicly (OpenAPI)? Can we try it in a sandbox now?\n- Which things can we create, read, update (PUT or PATCH?) and delete?\n- How are authentication, rate limits, versioning and breaking changes handled?\n- Do you support webhooks or events for real-time updates?\n\nOn **data**\n- Where is our data held, and who can access it? Is it ever used for training or analytics?\n- How do we get all of it out, in what format, at what cost?\n- What is your backup and recovery process, and when did you last test it?\n\nOn **security and compliance**\n- Cyber Essentials Plus or ISO 27001? When was the last independent penetration test, and can we see the summary?\n- Are you meeting WCAG 2.2 AA? Can we see your accessibility statement and audit?\n- What is your breach notification process?\n\nOn **delivery and change**\n- How often do you release, and how do we get notice? Is there a test environment for us to try releases before they go live?\n- Who are your other council customers, and may we speak to them?\n\nOn **commercials**\n- What is not included in this price? What happens at renewal?\n\n## Procurement in Scotland\n\nPublic contracts must follow procurement law, with routes such as **Public Contracts Scotland** for advertising opportunities and collaborative frameworks (for example through **Scotland Excel**, or the UK\'s Crown Commercial Service) that offer pre-approved suppliers and terms. The procurement route shapes what you can ask for and how much you can change. Involve procurement colleagues early, and write requirements around **outcomes and standards** (such as open APIs and exit rights) rather than brand names.\n\n::: try\nChoose a public supplier website with an API (for example Stripe, GOV.UK Notify or GOV.UK Pay, all of which publish their documentation). Open their developer documentation and find: how to authenticate, the list of endpoints, the status codes, the rate limits and the versioning policy. You will recognise every concept. Rate it out of ten for clarity. That is what "good" looks like.\n\nGOV.UK Notify documentation: `https://docs.notifications.service.gov.uk`\n:::\n\n::: repairs\nPick the three supplier answers that would worry you most if they were vague: probably data exit, API coverage and support hours for emergencies. Write the exact question you would ask for each, and the answer that would satisfy you.\n:::', 1, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
