INSERT INTO pm_learning_lessons (module_id, title, summary, content, sort_order, year_label, year_sort)
SELECT @mod, 'Capstone: follow one repair end to end', 'Trace a single repair through every layer of this course, and use the checklist to see how far you have come.', '## Putting it all together\n\nLet us follow **one repair** through everything you have learned, from a tenant\'s phone to the contractor\'s invoice. As you read, notice how many of the previous lessons light up.\n\n![A single repair passing from the tenant through the front end, API, back end, database, contractor and finance](local:repair-journey.svg)\n\n**1. The tenant reports a leak.** They open the council website on their phone. The **browser** asks **DNS** for the address, makes a secure **HTTPS** connection, and fetches **HTML, CSS and JavaScript** (the **front end**). The page is a **responsive website**, built from **design system** components, tested for **accessibility**.\n\n**2. The form is submitted.** The front end sends a **POST** request to the repairs **API**: `POST /repairs` with a **JSON** body containing the address, problem description and photos. A **token** in the header proves who is calling. The request travels as **packets** across routers.\n\n**3. The back end applies the rules.** It checks the data (**422** if the postcode is invalid), determines priority using the business rules, looks up the property by its **UPRN**, and checks for a vulnerability flag, a piece of **personal data** it will use only for a clear purpose. It saves the repair in the **database** and returns **201 Created** with the reference.\n\n**4. Integration.** A **webhook** or event tells the contractor\'s system there is a new job. The job appears in the contractor\'s **offline-capable app**, fetched with a **GET** to `/jobs?contractor=...`. The tenant receives an SMS confirmation through a notifications API.\n\n**5. The visit.** The operative arrives, uses the app (probably with poor signal), and records the work against the right **Schedule of Rates** codes. The app saves locally and **PATCHes** the job status when signal returns: `PATCH /repairs/4821` with `{"status": "completed"}`. Only the status changes, nothing else is overwritten.\n\n**6. Finance.** The completion triggers an automated check of the price against the rate. If it matches, payment is approved; if not, it goes to a person. An audit trail records who and what did each step.\n\n**7. Reporting and learning.** The data flows to a **data warehouse** and a dashboard shows first-time fix rates and response times, using definitions agreed in advance. Patterns of damp in one block are spotted early.\n\n**8. Behind the scenes.** All of this runs on **servers** in a **cloud** data centre as **SaaS**. Changes are built in **development**, checked in **test**, accepted in **UAT** by people who know repairs, and released through **CI/CD**. Security is maintained by **MFA**, **least privilege**, backups and monitoring. The supplier\'s **SLA** says what happens if any of it fails.\n\nNow think about what happens when any link breaks. If the **PATCH** accidentally became a **PUT**, fields would be lost. If the **certificate** expired, the app would stop. If the **webhook** failed, the contractor would never hear of the job. If the **data** were duplicated, two operatives would turn up. You can now diagnose *where* to look.\n\n## Your hero checklist\n\nTick these off in your own mind. Each is a skill you have built:\n\n- I can explain the **internet versus the web**, and what DNS, IP, packets and HTTPS do.\n- I can explain what a **browser** does and use **developer tools** to look at a page and a network request.\n- I can describe what an **API** is and read a **request and response**.\n- I know **GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE**, and why PUT versus PATCH matters.\n- I can read common **status codes** and know whether the fault is client or server.\n- I can read **JSON**.\n- I understand **authentication** (keys, tokens, OAuth) and **webhooks**.\n- I know the difference between **front end, back end and database**, and between a **monolith, microservices and a legacy system**.\n- I understand **environments, UAT and CI/CD**, and where my expertise fits in.\n- I know the **phases of delivery**, and how **design, accessibility and service design** shape services.\n- I understand why **data quality** and **UK GDPR** matter, and the basics of **cyber security**.\n- I can question a supplier on **SLAs, lock-in, APIs, data and security**.\n\n## What next?\n\nKeep going by doing. Ask to sit in on a supplier technical session. Read the system\'s API documentation. Look at the Network tab whenever something odd happens. Draw the system map. Run a UAT session. Stay curious: nobody in technology knows everything either. They just ask the next question.\n\nWhen you are ready for more, the follow-on course **Under the Bonnet: Going Deeper** covers the history from punched cards to quantum, memory and storage, how code runs, the tools and teams behind software, containers, the different kinds of database, race conditions and how banks keep money safe, getting the best from AI, and encryption, cyber security and open source.\n\nYou also have a rich resource on this site: the **Agile** and **discussion** areas, and the **Glossary** (use it whenever a word goes past you). Add comments below any lesson to share what you tried, what surprised you, and questions for the group.\n\n::: try\n**Final challenge: your own repair, end to end.** On a single page, trace a real repair type you know well (say, a no-heating emergency). Write the steps from report to payment. For each step note: which **system** holds the information, which **API call** or **integration** moves it on (and which **method** it would use), what could **go wrong**, and what **status code** or message would tell you. Share it with colleagues and the supplier. You will have created a better integration brief than most projects start with.\n:::\n\n::: remember\nYou did not need to become a developer. You needed to become an **informed customer and a confident translator** between the world of repairs and the world of technology. You are now both.\n:::', 2, NULL, NULL FROM DUAL WHERE @mod > 0;
SET @lesson := IF(@mod > 0, LAST_INSERT_ID(), 0);
